{
  "0": "For all four datasets, replace the baseline prediction loss with 0.5 times baseline loss plus 0.5 times mean softplus(negative_logit - positive_logit), using the existing sampled pairs and valid-position mask. Pairwise supervision may improve candidate ordering and validation HR@10 while retaining baseline calibration. Preserve sampling, architecture, scoring, optimizer and training schedule.",
  "1": "For all four datasets, replace dot-product compatibility with h^T W e, where W is one jointly trained square matrix initialized to identity. This lets SASRec learn which history-state dimensions should interact with candidate-item dimensions, potentially improving HR@10. Preserve the causal backbone, history readout, embeddings, loss and optimizer; save W and reproduce this scoring in the predictor writer.",
  "2": "For all four datasets, replace last-state readout with a fixed blend: 0.75 times the current causal state plus 0.25 times the recency-weighted mean of nonpadding causal states, with weights proportional to 0.9^age. At each training position, pool only its available prefix; use the same rule at inference. This may stabilize sparse-history interests. Preserve the backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer.",
  "3": "For all four datasets, reduce the baseline maximum context length to max(1, floor(0.75 times baseline length)), retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order. Shorter context may suppress stale interests and improve HR@10 without increasing compute. Use identical truncation and position indexing during training and prediction, including the predictor writer; preserve all other baseline settings and supplied splits."
}
